          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which conditions here require a second code, and does the order the two are listed in matter?",
          "answer": "The question mentions \"here\" but does not name any conditions, so I cannot tell which ones you mean. The rules depend on the specific conditions. If you share the names, I can check how each one is coded.\n\nThe coding guidance I found gives the general pattern. The ICD-10-CM conventions pair \"code first\" with \"use additional code.\" These fix the order: the underlying condition is reported first, and the manifestation follows. \"Code also\" means two codes are needed but does not set which comes first. (Source: ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions.)\n\nThree examples in the corpus show this:\n\n- **Hypertensive heart disease with heart failure:** The combination code for the heart disease is reported first. A heart failure code is then added to show the type of heart failure. The heart failure code is added, not swapped in. (Source: Coding heart failure: I50.)\n- **Type 2 diabetes with chronic kidney disease:** The combination code names the link. A separate code for the stage of kidney disease is added for detail. (Source: Coding type 2 diabetes: E11.)\n- **Type 2 diabetes with a foot ulcer:** The combination code is paired with a code for the ulcer's site and depth. (Source: Coding type 2 diabetes: E11.)\n\nOn whether order matters: where the conventions say \"code first\" or \"use additional code,\" the order is fixed, so it does matter. Where a note says only \"code also,\" the corpus does not say that order matters.\n\nThe corpus does not give a full list of which conditions need a second code, so I can't give you one. Coding decisions are normally made by a trained coder or the clinician who wrote the record, using the full classification.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
